vanced zyLab format using the “Clone“ button at the top of labs.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                ZyBooks maintained labs (ZMLs) in the Programming in Java, Python, C, and C++ books can be toggled to use the classic or advanced labs version.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n\\n        "],[8,[39,1],null,[["@additionalInfo","@availability","@imageAltText","@imageFile","@new","@title"],[true,"Available in: All books","A simplified representation of a class roster.","class_roster.png",true,"Class roster"]],[["default"],[[[[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                The class roster was designed to help instructors answer two key questions: \\"Who is in my class?\\" and \\"How are they doing?\\"\\n                The roster makes it easy to view a list of the students in the course, as well as their performance.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[10,"ul"],[12],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Measure performance with activity completion and time spent metrics (according to participation, challenge, and zyLab activities)."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Sort by any column."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"View metrics by chapter or section."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Download custom views as a .csv file."],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                Access the class roster in the reporting tab of any zyBook.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n\\n        "],[8,[39,1],null,[["@additionalInfo","@availability","@imageAltText","@imageFile","@new","@title"],[true,"Available in: Computer Systems and Assembly Programming","A simplified representation of a register file.","csap_zylabs.png",true,"Computer Systems and Assembly Programming zyLabs"]],[["default"],[[[[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                To help students understand the basic constructs of assembly programs in MIPSzy (a simplified version of MIPS using a small subset of the MIPS instructions and a register file with only 8 primary registers),\\n                we have created 10 zyLabs ranging in difficulty from writing simple instructions of arithmetic expressions and memory accesses to implementing complex procedures.\\n                Although the zyLabs are written in MIPSzy, instructors can use the same system to create auto-scored labs in MIPS.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n\\n            With the assistance of the built-in MIPS simulator, students can:\\n            "],[10,"ul"],[12],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Step through the program execution."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Gain insight into how the registers and memory interact with the program during execution."],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n\\n        "],[8,[39,1],null,[["@availability","@imageAltText","@imageFile","@learnMoreLink","@title"],["Available in: All books with zyLabs except MATLAB","An outline of a section with a short answer question in the center.","similarity_checker_known_solution.png","https://support.zybooks.com/hc/en-us/articles/360038739093-zyLabs-SimChecker","Upload known solutions in similarity checker"]],[["default"],[[[[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                Our similarity checker for zyLab submissions has been updated with\\n                a feature to upload and test similarity against known solutions.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n            This means instructors can check the similarity between their students\' code and:\\n            "],[10,"ul"],[12],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Solutions from an answer key."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Suggestions from other online sources."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Previous class submissions."],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                Try testing against known solutions by visiting the \\"Similarity detection\\" section under a zyLab.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n    "],[13],[1,"\\n    "],[10,0],[14,0,"box"],[12],[1,"\\n        "],[10,"h1"],[14,0,"section-header primary-font-semibold"],[12],[1,"Beta programs"],[13],[1,"\\n"],[1,"\\n        "],[8,[39,1],null,[["@additionalInfo","@availability","@imageAltText","@imageFile","@new","@requestAccess","@title"],[true,"Available in: Digital Design","A simplified representation of a circuit.","verilog_zylabs.png",true,true,"Verilog zyLabs"]],[["default"],[[[[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                To supplement our Digital Design zyBook, we have developed a new zyLabs platform that supports Verilog zyLabs.\\n                The zyLabs are designed to teach behavioral and structural design and equip students to implement a range of designs from simple combinational circuits to more complex FSMs.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n\\n            Key innovations of the Verilog zyLabs include:\\n            "],[10,"ul"],[12],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Support for auto-grading."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Ability to provide immediate feedback to students when errors exist in their code."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"A waveform viewer that will enable students to view their signals to better understand the timing and functionality of their implementation."],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n\\n        "],[8,[39,1],null,[["@additionalInfo","@availability","@imageAltText","@imageFile","@new","@requestAccess","@title"],[true,"Available in: Computer Organization and Design (all versions)","A simplified representation of a MIPS architecture processor.","cod_mips_zylabs.png",true,true,"Computer Organization and Design zyLabs"]],[["default"],[[[[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"To help students understand the basic constructs of MIPS, ARM, or RISC-V assembly programs, we have created 10 zyLabs ranging in difficulty from writing simple instructions of arithmetic expressions and memory accesses to implementing complex procedures."],[13],[1,"\\n\\n            With the assistance of the built-in MIPS, ARM, or RISC-V simulator, students can:\\n            "],[10,"ul"],[12],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Step through the program execution."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Gain insight into how the registers and memory interact with the program during execution."],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"Reach out to your sales representative for access to MIPS and ARM labs. Use the \\"request access\\" button below to try out the RISC-V labs beta."],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n\\n        "],[8,[39,1],null,[["@availability","@imageAltText","@imageFile","@learnMoreLink","@requestAccess","@title"],["Available in: All books with zyLabs except MATLAB","An outline of a code stub above an outline of a grading rubric.","zyLabs manual grading.png","https://support.zybooks.com/hc/en-us/articles/1260802687050-zyLabs-submission-review-manual-grading-beta-",true,"zyLabs manual scoring"]],[["default"],[[[[1,"\\n            "],[10,2],[12],[1,"\\n                The purpose of this beta is two-fold.\\n                The first goal is to provide an efficient experience allowing instructors to quickly review each student\'s latest, highest scoring submission on each zyLab.\\n                The second goal is to provide 2 new test types allowing instructors to add scores manually by adding or deducting points to the results of the automated testing results.\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n\\n            Two manual test types are supported:\\n            "],[10,"ul"],[12],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Manual score test - This is an additive test type. If you\'ve set 50 points worth of automated tests and add a manual score test worth 10 points, students will only be able to achieve 50 of 60 points before you add a manual score of 0 - 10 points."],[13],[1,"\\n                "],[10,"li"],[12],[1,"Manual deduction test - This test type may only subtract points. If you\'ve set 50 points worth of automated tests and manual deduction of maximum -10 points, students may achieve 50 points on the automated tests and you may choose to remove up to 10 points when reviewing their submission."],[13],[1,"\\n            "],[13],[1,"\\n        "]],[]]]]],[1,"\\n    "],[13],[1,"\\n"],[13],[1,"\\n"]],[],false,["page-title","whats-new-blurb"]]',moduleName:"zybooks-web/whats-new/template.hbs",isStrictMode:!1})
